1. MCP Server refers to the program that serves context data, regardless of where it runs. 
2. AI Host, in addition to being a host, managing MCP clients, can also spawn/manage local MCP servers. Like Claude Desktop launching the filesystem serve.
3. MCP Gateway should be :
    a. controlled
    b. configurable
    c. observable
    c. policy aware
    d. secure
    e. deterministic
    f. multi-tenant
    g. enterprise-usable 
    h. easily deployable
    i. easily integratable
    j. safe-by-default, least-privege oriented
    k. auditable
    l. governable
    m. reliable 
    n. scalable
    o. extensible 
    p. portable 
    q. low-friction
    r. traceable 
    s. compliant
    t. version-aware 
    u. resilient
    v. cost aware 


My basic first stage:
1. I should be able to demo that an AI host is able to get through my gateway to an MCP server. 
AI Host → MCP Gateway → Neo4j MCP Server, with some basic features in play from the gateway like:
a. auth
b. basic policy
c. logging 
d. clean config 
e. metrics and its dashboards
f. tracing 
g. security control 
h. basic UI
Each item could be thin but i will have all these from day1. 
